import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class Fibbo {

    private static final Map<Integer, Integer> FIBBONACCI_CACHE = new HashMap<>();

    static {
        FIBBONACCI_CACHE.put(0, 0);
        FIBBONACCI_CACHE.put(1, 1);
    }

    public static int next(int num) {
        if (num < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input must be non-negative");
        }

        if (FIBBONACCI_CACHE.containsKey(num)) {
            return FIBBONACCI_CACHE.get(num);
        }

        int result = next(num - 1) + next(num - 2);
        FIBBONACCI_CACHE.put(num, result);
        return result;
    }
}
```

This class uses a static map to cache previously computed Fibonacci numbers, which helps improve performance for larger inputs. The `next` method checks if the input number is already in the cache, and if not, it recursively computes the next Fibonacci number using the formula `F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2)`. The method also checks if the input is non-negative, as the Fibonacci sequence is not defined for negative numbers.
