T factorial(T n) {
    if constexpr (std::is_integral_v<T>) {
        T result = 1;
        for (T i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
            result *= i;
        }
        return result;
    } else {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Non-integral types are not supported.");
    }
}

int main() {
    std::cout << factorial(5) << std::endl; // Output: 120
    std::cout << factorial(3.14) << std::endl; // Compiler error: Non-integral types are not supported.

    struct MyComplex {
        double real, imag;
        friend auto operator*(const MyComplex& lhs, const MyComplex& rhs) {
            return MyComplex{lhs.real * rhs.real - lhs.imag * rhs.imag, lhs.real * rhs.imag + lhs.imag * rhs.real};
        }
    };

    MyComplex c1{2.0, 3.0};
    MyComplex c2{4.0, 5.0};
    std::cout << factorial(c1 * c2) << std::endl; // Compiler error: No instance of function template "factorial" matches the argument list.

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `factorial` function is only applicable to types that support multiplication, as defined by the `Multiplicable` concept. This ensures that the function can only be used with types that have a well-defined multiplication operation, such as integers or custom complex numbers. The example demonstrates that the function will not compile when called with inappropriate types, such as floating-point numbers or user-defined types without a multiplication operator.

