 Yes, you can create a custom Pydantic model that automatically truncates string fields if they are too long. To achieve this, you can use Pydantic's `validator` decorator to define a custom validation method that truncates the string if it exceeds a specified length.

Here's an example of how you can do this:

```python
from pydantic import BaseModel, validator, Field

class TruncatedStringModel(BaseModel):
    max_length: int = 10  # Default maximum length for truncation

    def truncate_string(cls, value: str, max_length: int) -> str:
        if len(value) > max_length:
            return value[:max_length]
        return value

    @validator('*', pre=True, always=True)
    def truncate_fields(cls, value, field):
        if isinstance(value, str) and hasattr(cls, 'max_length'):
            return cls.truncate_string(value, cls.max_length)
        return value

class MyModel(TruncatedStringModel):
    name: str
    description: str

# Example usage
data = {
    'name': 'This is a very long name that should be truncated',
    'description': 'This is a very long description that should be truncated'
}

model = MyModel(**data)
print(model)
```

In this example:

1. `TruncatedStringModel` is a base class that includes a `truncate_string` method to truncate strings and a `truncate_fields` validator that applies this truncation to all string fields.
2. `MyModel` extends `TruncatedStringModel` and defines the fields `name` and `description`.
3. The `truncate_fields` validator is applied to all fields (`'*'`) and checks if the value is a string. If it is, it truncates the string to the specified `max_length`.

When you create an instance of `MyModel` with data that exceeds the `max_length`, the strings will be automatically truncated.

You can adjust the `max_length` attribute in the `TruncatedStringModel` class to set the desired maximum length for truncation. If you want different fields to have different maximum lengths, you can modify the `truncate_fields` validator to check the field name and apply different truncation lengths accordingly.

Here's a C++ solution using `<unordered_map>` for efficient character counting and `<algorithm>` for array manipulation. This solution is case-insensitive and optimized for performance.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>

int main() {
    char Arr[] = {'h', 'e', 'L', 'I', 'o', '*', '7', 'W', 'O', 'r', 'L', '#', 'd', '-', '5'};
    std::unordered_map<char, int> charCount;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> symCount;
    char symArr[15];
    int symIdx = 0;

    // Count characters and symbols
    for (char c : Arr) {
        if (std::isalpha(c)) {
            charCount[std::tolower(c)]++;
        } else if (!std::isalnum(c)) {
            symArr[symIdx++] = c;
            symCount[c]++;
        }
    }

    // Print unique symbols
    std::cout << "Unique symbols: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < symIdx; ++i) {
        std::cout << symArr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    // Print letters that appear only once
    std::cout << "Letters that appear only once: ";
    for (char c : Arr) {
        if (std::isalpha(c) && charCount[std::tolower(c)] == 1) {
            std::cout << c << " ";
            charCount[std::tolower(c)] = 0; // Mark as printed
        }
    }
    std::cout << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

This code will output:

```
Unique symbols: * # -
Letters that appear only once: h e W r d
```

 To achieve the tasks you've described, you can use the following C++ code. This code will extract all symbols from the array `Arr` into a new array `symArr`, and it will print all letters (both capital and small) that appear only once in `Arr` (case insensitive).

