To read YAML configurations in Java, you can use a library called SnakeYAML. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. First, add the SnakeYAML dependency to your project. If you're using Maven, add this to your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.yaml</groupId>
        <artifactId>snakeyaml</artifactId>
        <version>1.29</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

If you're using Gradle, add this to your `build.gradle`:

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.yaml:snakeyaml:1.29'
}
```

2. Create a YAML file. For example, create a file named `config.yaml`:

```yaml
name: Test
age: 30
```

3. Now, you can read this YAML file in Java:

```java
import org.yaml.snakeyaml.Yaml;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Yaml yaml = new Yaml();
        InputStream inputStream = Main.class.getClassLoader().getResourceAsStream("config.yaml");
        Map<String, Object> obj = yaml.load(inputStream);
        System.out.println(obj);
    }
}
```

This will print:

```
{name=Test, age=30}
```

4. If you have a complex YAML file and you want to map it to a Java object, you can do so by creating a Java class that matches the structure of your YAML file. For example, if your YAML file looks like this:

```yaml
name: Test
age: 30
address:
  street: Example Street
  city: Example City
```

You can create a Java class like this:

```java
public class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;
    private Address address;

    // getters and setters
}

public class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;

    // getters and setters
}
```

